{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Baltimore Orioles opened the season with genuine promise, riding a powerful lineup and the early heroics of Mark Trumbo and J.J. Hardy to a blistering 15-8 April. The power was real — Baltimore would slug 232 home runs on the year — and for a few weeks Camden Yards felt like a place where big things could happen. But the cracks in the pitching staff were already forming, with relievers Brad Brach and Donnie Hart giving back leads in late April, foreshadowing the bullpen fragility that would haunt the club all summer.\n\nMay and June proved a grind, the team going 12-16 in each month as a rotation anchored by the unreliable Ubaldo Jiménez kept bleeding runs. Yet the offense refused to quit. Welington Castillo emerged as an unlikely power source, delivering back-to-back massive home runs in late May, and Trey Mancini announced himself with a two-homer game against Pittsburgh in early June. Jonathan Schoop was blossoming into an All-Star, and a Castillo grand slam on June 14 represented everything this team could be when the bats and the breaks aligned.\n\nJuly brought a similar 12-14 ledger but the tide was quietly shifting. August, improbably, was the team's best month at 17-12, fueled almost entirely by Manny Machado turning in one of the great individual performances in recent Orioles history. His grand slam on August 18 — the highest single-play swing of the entire season at +0.684 WPA — was the centerpiece of a personal tear that saw him launch 28 home runs by month's end, single-handedly keeping Baltimore within range of a wild card.\n\nSeptember destroyed whatever hope Machado had kept alive. The Orioles went 7-20 in the final month, and a September 5 Machado two-run homer against the Yankees — worth a remarkable 0.893 WPA — felt more like a eulogy than a rally cry. The pitching surrendered run after run, and Miguel Castro's late September implosion against Boston, allowing two runs on a single to blow a game, was emblematic of a club running on fumes. The season that briefly looked like a playoff contender ended at 75-87, a reminder that homers and heart can only carry a team so far when the arms give way.",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "ch1",
      "title": "Hot Out of the Gate",
      "description": "Baltimore storms through April with powerful hitting and an early Trumbo blast setting the tone for a 15-8 start.",
